data.content:
## Migration to .NET 8

Your code has three patterns that need replacing:

| Pattern | Issue in .NET 8 |
|---------|----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware |

Additionally, there's a **race condition**: multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals — only one item gets dequeued.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — exit cleanly
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();               // signals the loop to exit
        _thread?.Join(5000);         // wait for graceful shutdown
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### What Changed

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) |
|--------------------------|----------------|
| `ManualResetEvent` + manual `lock`/`Queue` | `BlockingCollection<string>` (thread-safe, blocks efficiently) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` to wake blocked thread | `CancellationToken` passed to `GetConsumingEnumerable` |
| `Thread.Abort()` as fallback kill | Removed — cooperative cancellation is sufficient |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` (implicit) | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Race condition losing queued items | `BlockingCollection` drains all items correctly |

---

### Key Pitfalls

1. **`Thread.Abort` simply doesn't work** — it throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime. There is no forced-kill alternative in managed code.

2. **If `Handle()` is long-running**, pass the token into it:
   ```csharp
   private void Handle(string item, CancellationToken token)
   {
       // Check periodically in expensive work
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
   }
   ```

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.

4. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** lower in the call chain — only catch it at the top-level loop.

5. **If you need a hard timeout** on shutdown:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop()
   {
       _cts.Cancel();
       if (!_thread?.Join(5000) ?? true)
       {
           // Log warning — thread didn't exit in time.
           // Only Process.Kill on a child process can force-stop uncooperative code.
       }
   }
   ```

---

### Alternative: Channel-based (fully async)

If your codebase is async-first, prefer `Channel<string>`:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start() => _runTask = Task.Run(RunLoopAsync);

    private async Task RunLoopAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(_cts.Token))
            {
                await HandleAsync(item, _cts.Token);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.Cancel();
        if (_runTask is not null) await _runTask;
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

The `BlockingCollection` version is the closest 1:1 migration. The `Channel<T>` version is idiomatic modern .NET for new code.
